# 38. COMMON XSS MISTAKES

### Mistake 1

"Filtering `<script>` prevents XSS."

❌ Not necessarily.

Different contexts and HTML/DOM behaviors require proper defenses.

---

### Mistake 2

"HTTPS prevents XSS."

❌ False.

HTTPS protects data in transit; it does not automatically make unsafe HTML rendering secure.

---

### Mistake 3

"CSP means we don't need output encoding."

❌ False.

CSP is defense-in-depth.

---

### Mistake 4

"React/Angular/Vue means XSS is impossible."

❌ False.

Developers can bypass framework protections.

---

### Mistake 5

"Every XSS can steal cookies."

❌ False.

Cookie flags such as HttpOnly and application/browser protections can change the impact.

---

# 39. SQLi VS XSS VS COMMAND INJECTION

| Attack | Target |
|---|---|
| SQL Injection | Database/interpreter |
| XSS | Browser/client-side execution |
| Command Injection | OS command interpreter |

### Memory

```text
SQLi → SQL
XSS  → Browser
CMDi → OS
```

---

# 40. XSS VS CSRF

| XSS | CSRF |
|---|---|
| Injected content/script executes in browser context | Tricks browser into sending an unwanted request |
| Usually requires unsafe content handling | Often relies on existing authentication/session |
| Output encoding is important defense | CSRF tokens/SameSite and other controls help |
| Can potentially enable actions as victim | Causes unauthorized actions under victim's session |

### Important

XSS can sometimes undermine defenses against other web attacks, so fixing XSS is important.

